Step 1: Understand the Project Requirements
Login Page: You need to create a login page that authenticates users using either their username or email and password.
Profile Page: After logging in, the user should see their profile page with at least three pieces of information (e.g., XP amount, grades, skills, etc.).
Statistics Section: You must create at least two SVG-based graphs to visualize data (e.g., XP over time, audit ratio, etc.).
Hosting: Once the project is complete, you need to host it on a platform like GitHub Pages, Netlify, etc.

Step 2: Set Up Your React Project
Install Dependencies
axios or fetch for making API requests.
react-router-dom for routing (login page, profile page).
jwt-decode to decode the JWT token.
Any SVG library or charting library if you don’t want to manually create SVG graphs.

Step 3: Create the Login Page
Design the Login Form:
Create a form with fields for username/email and password.
Add a submit button to trigger the login process.
Handle Authentication:
When the form is submitted, make a POST request to the signin endpoint (https://((DOMAIN))/api/auth/signin) using Basic Authentication.
Encode the username/email and password in Base64 and send it in the request headers.
If successful, the API will return a JWT token. Store this token securely (e.g., in localStorage or sessionStorage).
If authentication fails, display an error message to the user.
Logout Functionality:
Provide a logout button that clears the stored JWT token and redirects the user to the login page.

Step 4: Fetch Data from the GraphQL API
Set Up GraphQL Queries:
Write GraphQL queries to fetch the data you need which are Basic User Identification, XP Amount, Skills and audit.
Use tools like GraphiQL or Postman to test your queries before implementing them in your app.
Make Authenticated Requests:
Use the stored JWT token to authenticate your GraphQL requests.
Include the token in the Authorization header as a Bearer token.
Use fetch to send the GraphQL queries to the endpoint (https://((DOMAIN))/api/graphql-engine/v1/graphql).

Step 5: Design the Profile Page
Display User Information:
Use the data fetched from the GraphQL API to display at least three pieces of information (e.g., username, XP amount, skills, etc.).
Organize the information in a clean and visually appealing way.
Create the Statistics Section:
Use SVG to create at least two graphs. Examples:
A line chart showing XP earned over time.
A bar chart showing PASS/FAIL ratios for projects.
Ensure the graphs are interactive or animated if possible.
UI Design:
Follow good UI/UX principles (e.g., consistent spacing, readable fonts, intuitive navigation).
Use a CSS framework like Bootstrap or Tailwind if needed.

Step 6: Implement SVG Graphs
Choose Graph Types:
Decide on the types of graphs you want to create (e.g., line chart, bar chart, pie chart).
Create SVG Elements:
Use SVG elements like <line>, <rect>, <circle>, and <text> to build your graphs.
Use JavaScript to dynamically update the graph data based on the fetched information.
Add Interactivity:
Add hover effects, tooltips, or animations to make the graphs more engaging.

Step 7: Handle Routing
Set Up Routes:
Use react-router-dom to create routes for the login page and profile page.
Protect the profile page so that only authenticated users can access it.
Redirect Users:
Redirect users to the profile page after successful login.
Redirect users to the login page if they try to access the profile page without being authenticated.

Step 8: Test Your Application
Test Authentication:
Ensure the login page works with both username and email.
Test error handling for invalid credentials.
Test GraphQL Queries:
Verify that all required data is fetched and displayed correctly.
Test UI/UX:
Check that the profile page is responsive and looks good on different screen sizes.
Ensure the graphs are functional and visually appealing.

Step 9: Host Your Application
Choose a Hosting Platform:
Options include GitHub Pages, Netlify, Vercel, etc.
Deploy Your App:
Build your React app using npm run build.
Follow the hosting platform’s instructions to deploy your app.
Test the Live Version:
Ensure everything works as expected in the live environment.